message.content:
Based on my analysis of the panoramic radiograph — examining the apical regions of the dentition under multiple contrast/sharpen enhancements — I can report localized periapical radiolucent changes. I want to be candid: this radiograph is low in diagnostic sharpness (blurring, heavy metal-restoration artifacts on the patient's right), so the periapical findings are subtle rather than striking, and my confidence per site is moderate.

The regions I judged to show periapical bone change (regional mineral loss / ill-defined radiolucency at the apices) are: the lower-right molar apices (crowned tooth), the lower-left molar apices near the malposed tooth, and the apices of the upper-right root-treated teeth. None reach a well-corticated, frankly cystic appearance, so all are graded PAI 3.

